    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for the breakdown. I'm a bit concerned about the potential for chaos if we go with a system that isn't precise enough. If we have a large influx of miscellaneous papers that don't fit perfectly into a designated category, how do we prevent the collection from becoming a disorganized mess? I want to avoid a situation where items just end up lost or misfiled in a way that makes the archive unmanageable.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To mitigate this risk, you may consider implementing a hybrid approach that combines elements of both DDC and LCC. Alternatively, you could also consider using a third system such as the RDA (Resource Description and Access) framework, which is designed to provide a more flexible and adaptable way of classifying and describining individual items. Additionally, implementing clear templates and guidelines for cataloging and processing miscellaneous papers can help ensure consistency and organization within the collection.",
      "source": "target",
